1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Bb4 5. O-O O-O 6. d3 d6 7. Bg5 Bxc3 8.
bxc3 Qe7 9. Re1 h6 10. Bc1 Bd7 {[Mr. Blake deliberately adopted this move,
which is not the best. He stated that Mr. Jacobs had followed so closely the
play of the now fashionable variation at the Vienna tournament in the game
Teichmann v Cohn that he determined to leave the beaten track in order to
throw Mr. Jacobs upon his own resources - BCM]} 11. d4 Rad8 12. Ba3 Rfe8 13.
Qd3 Nh5 14. Bxc6 Nf4 15. Qe3 Bxc6 16. dxe5 Ng6 17. exd6 cxd6 18. Rad1 Qxe4 19.
Qxe4 Bxe4 20. Nd4 Bd5 21. Nb3 Nf4 {[This and the next move turn the position
in Black's favour]} 22. f3 Re2 23. Rxe2 Nxe2+ 24. Kf2 Nxc3 25. Rd3 Nb5 26. Rxd5
Nxa3 27. Ra5 Nxc2 28. Rxa7 Rb8 29. Na5 b5 30. Nc6 Rb6 31. Ne7+ Kh7 32. Nd5 Rc6
33. Rxf7 Ra6 34. h4 Rxa2 35. Kg3 Kg8 36. Rb7 Ne1 37. Nf4 g5 {[This is not good,
and might easily have had serious consequences to Mr. Blake's chance of tieing
with Mr. Atkins.]} 38. hxg5 hxg5 39. Ne6 Rxg2+ 40. Kh3 Rb2 41. Kg4 Rg2+ 42. Kh3
{[Mr. Jacobs was hard pressed for time at this stage, and this probably
accounts for the oversight.]} Rg1 43. Kh2 $4 (43. Nd4 $17) 43... Nxf3+ 44. Kh3
g4# {Source: BCM, Sept 1909, p414} 0-1

